source, which might be thermalized. The scattering and collective expansion which is driven by internal pressure gradients introduce a blast velocity. This blast (hydro-like) velocity combined with the (thermal or kinetic) freeze-out temperature, describes the shape of the transverse momentum spectra, which depends on the mass of the measured identified particle [27]. The corrected invariant spectra presented in this chapter can be written as,

\[\frac{1}{N_{\rm event}}\frac{d^{2}N}{2\pi p_{\rm T}dp_{\rm T}dy}=\frac{1}{N_{ \rm event}}\times\frac{1}{2\pi p_{\rm T}}\times\frac{1}{\delta p_{\rm T} \delta y}\times\frac{1}{C(p_{\rm T})}\times Y(p_{\rm T}) \tag{3.17}\]

where \(Y(p_{\rm T})\) and \(C(p_{\rm T})\) are, respectively, raw spectra and correction factors.

Figure 3.12 and 3.13 show the \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra of \(d\) and \(\overline{d}\), respectively, for Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) = 39, 27, 19.6, 11.5 and 7.7 GeV. \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra are measured at mid-rapidity (\(|y|<0.3\)) and for eight centrality bins. In Fig. 3.12 and Fig. 3.13, spectra of centrality bins are scaled by powers of 10 for better visibility. Curves shown on the spectra represent Blast-wave (defined later in Eq. 3.20) fit to the spectra. The Blast-wave fit is used to extract yield at unmeasured \(p_{\rm T}\) ranges where there is no data points. Blast-wave function is also used to calculate \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\). Other functional forms were also used to fit the \(p_{\rm T}\)-spectra are given in Eq. 3.21. The fit functions in Eq. 3.21, were used to calculate systematic error on the integrated yield and \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\). The shape of both \(d\) and \(\overline{d}\) spectra seems to be hardening for increasing centrality. As central collisions produce more particles, collective effects should be stronger and therefore, heavier particles should be boosted to higher transverse momenta. The spectra can be further studied by looking at \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) which is presented in the following subsection.

#### Average Transverse Momentum \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\)

The change in the measured spectral shapes can be quantitatively characterized by the average transverse momentum (\(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\)) in terms of energy and centrality. In general, it provides the information on the transverse dynamics of different types of particles.

Figure 3.12: Centrality dependence of \(d\)-spectra measured in mid-rapidity (\(|y|<0.3\)) in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) = 39, 27, 19.6, 11.5, 7.7 GeV. Spectra are shown in eight centrality bins and are scaled for different collision centrality for clarity. Curves represent Blast-wave fit to the data points. Error bars corresponds to quadrature sum of statistical and systematic error.

We calculate \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) using the following formula,

\[\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle=\frac{\int p_{\rm T}2\pi p_{\rm T}f(p_{\rm T})dp_{\rm T} }{\int 2\pi p_{\rm T}f(p_{\rm T})dp_{\rm T}}, \tag{3.18}\]

where \(f(p_{\rm T})\) are the fit functions used for \(d\) and \(\overline{d}\) as mentioned earlier and the integration is over 0-10 GeV/c.

Figure 3.13: Centrality dependence of \(\overline{d}\)-spectra measured in mid-rapidity (\(|y|<0.3\)) in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=39\), 27, 19.6 and 11.5 GeV. Spectra for \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=39\), 27 GeV are scaled for different collision centrality for clarity. Due to low particle statistics, \(\overline{d}\) spectra is shown for only one centrality in \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=19.6\) and 11.5 GeV. Curves represent Blast-wave fit to the data points. Error bars corresponds to quadrature sum of statistical and systematic error.

#### Systematic Uncertainties on \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\)

Systematic uncertainties on \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) depend on the extrapolation of spectra. It is estimated by using the various functional forms used for extrapolation of the \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra (Eq. 3.20, Eq. 3.21.). For deuteron, an additional systematic uncertainty on \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) due to the \(p_{\rm T}\)-dependent \(d\) background from the beam pipe. The systematic error due to these sources estimated and included in quadrature to the total systematic uncertainties. The total uncertainties on \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) are \(\sim\)14%, and \(\sim\)19% for \(d\) and \(\overline{d}\) respectively. Figure 3.14 shows the mid-rapidity \(\langle p_{\rm T}\rangle\) of \(d\) and \(\overline{d}\) as a function of \(\langle N_{\rm part}\rangle\) for Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) = 39, 27, 19.6, 11.5 and 7.7 GeV.

