\(p\) and \(\overline{p}\) spectra in [49] is not feed-down corrected for weak decay protons. Therefore, we have calculated fractions of weak decay \(p\) and \(\overline{p}\) from all possible particles (e.g. \(\Lambda\), \(\Xi\)) produced in experiment and removed the contribution from the inclusive \(p\) and \(\overline{p}\) spectra in [49]. The procedure is explained in detail in the Appendix at the end of this chapter. We have used weak decay corrected \(p\) and \(\overline{p}\) spectra for the observables presented in this chapter. The following subsections shows the measurements of \(B_{2}\) (\(\overline{B}_{2}\)) and the corresponding physics discussion.

#### 3.6.4.1 Centrality Dependence of \(B_{2}\) and \(\overline{B}_{2}\)

Figure 3.18 shows the centrality dependence of \(B_{2}\) and \(\overline{B}_{2}\) for Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) = 39, 27, 19.6, 11.5 and 7.7 GeV. As seen from Fig. 3.18, both \(B_{2}\) and \(\overline{B}_{2}\) decreases with increasing centrality for all beam energies. The correlation volume (\(V_{f}\)) is inversely proportional to \(B_{2}(\overline{B}_{2})\). Therefore, from Fig. 3.18, we can see that the correlation volume (\(V_{f}\)) increases with increasing centrality. The correlation volume (\(V_{f}\)) of \(d\) also shows a slight energy dependence, it increases with increasing beam energy studied. Although \(B_{2}\) shows energy dependence, \(\overline{B}_{2}\) one the other hand is independent of energy. The centrality dependence of \(\overline{B}_{2}\) is similar as \(B_{2}\). Therefore,

