#### Calculation of Feed-down Ratio

To quantify the amount of weak-decay \(p\) (\(\overline{p}\)) in the inclusive \(p\) (\(\overline{p}\)) spectra, we define the feed-down ratio as,

\[\mathrm{FD}_{\mathrm{ratio}}(p_{\mathrm{T}})\ =\ \frac{\mathrm{weak\ decay\ p(\overline{p})}}{ \mathrm{inclusive\ p(\overline{p})}} \tag{3.22}\]

The \(p\), \(\overline{p}\) spectra in [49] was measured at mid-rapidity (\(|y|<0.1\)). Therefore, we select the daughter \(p(\overline{p})\) which are within \(|y|<0.1\) which constitutes the numerator of the equation 3.22. The Denominator is the inclusive \(p(\overline{p})\) from [49]. Figure 3.24 showsthe FD ratio of \(p\) and \(\overline{p}\) for \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}\) = 39, 27, 19.6, 11.5 and 7.7 GeV. The error bars corresponds to the systematic error. As seen from Fig. 3.24, the FD ratio is highest for lowest \(p_{\rm T}\) bin. Then the FD ratio decreases as a function of \(p_{\rm T}\). We also observe that the FD ratio is higher for \(\overline{p}\) than \(p\) and this difference increases as beam energy decreases.

#### Systematic Error on Feed-Down Ratio

The \(\Lambda\) (\(\overline{\Lambda}\)) spectra is measured experimentally in the \(p_{\rm T}\) range \(0.5<p_{\rm T}<2.5\) GeV/c. Therefore, \(\Lambda\) (\(\overline{\Lambda}\)) spectra has been fitted with fit function to get the spectra in the unmeasured region. The \(m_{\rm T}\)-exponential fit function is used as default fit function to extrapolate the \(p_{\rm T}\) distribution in the unmeasured region. However, other fit functions (Blast-wave and \(p_{\rm T}\)-exponential function) has been also used to calculate the systematic error due to extrapolation. Moreover, we have also varied the amount of feed-down from \(\Sigma\) (\(\overline{\Sigma}\)) and used the results to calculate the systematic error. The systematic error of \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) spectra has also been used to calculated the systematic error on the feed-down ratio. Table 3.13 and Table 3.14 lists the FD ratios along with the systematic error. These FD ratios were used in this analysis to correct the inclusive \(p(\overline{p})\) spectra.

